1) I initially stored event lineups in fixed artist columns in Supabase. It seemed simpler, until maintaining relationships and querying artists became painful. I moved to a normalized event_artists junction table with foreign keys, then fixed the permissions needed for public reads. I learned that a quick schema is only simple until the product grows.

2) I went beyond my research responsibilities to build and deploy an open-source platform for two behavioral studies, Face Reconstruction and Minority Overestimation, using Streamlit and JATOS. What started as a personal initiative became shared infrastructure for the entire research team, simplifying experiment deployment and making our workflows more accessible and reproducible.

3) I used to ask LLMs for whole-file rewrites while building React projects. I found those changes harder to review and sometimes introduced unrelated errors. Now I use them for scoped changes, inspect the diff, run TypeScript checks and builds, and commit incrementally. The goal shifted from generating more code to keeping control of the system.

4) As a student leader, I helped coordinate events of up to 2,500 people and budgets near €100K. In engineering, I apply the same discipline: define ownership, separate concerns, make data flows explicit, document decisions, and verify changes before merging. I see systems work as making complexity legible enough that others can build on it.
